# THE REAL, DEPLOYED LAMBDA'S OWN PERSISTENCE OVERLAY —
# `Adapters::Folder#load_domain`'s own `environment:` mechanism
# (Runtime::Loader.boot's own comment has the full provenance): loaded
# LAST, merged into `quality_control.world`'s own `persisted_by`
# binding rather than replacing the whole world file, exactly so this
# file needs to know nothing about anything ELSE that base file
# declares (`realm`, `deployed_to("AwsLambda")`, ...).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL: `quality_control.world`'s own `database
# "hecks_quality_control"` is a BARE dbname — deliberately, per that
# file's own header, resolving through the LOCAL Postgres socket the
# same way `psql hecks_quality_control` already would. That is exactly
# right for local dev and CI, and exactly wrong for a real, deployed
# Lambda: there is no local socket inside a Lambda execution
# environment, only the real RDS endpoint `qa/lambda_handler.rb`
# already composed into `ENV["DATABASE_URL"]` at cold start (fetched
# from Secrets Manager, never typed or committed — see that file's own
# header). `Hecks.boot(path, environment: "production")` is what picks
# this file up; `qa/lambda_handler.rb`'s own boot call passes that.
#
# `ENV.fetch("DATABASE_URL")`, not a hardcoded string — evaluated as
# ordinary Ruby the moment this file loads, the identical mechanism
# `bin/project_deploy`'s own mint-era boot check already uses to pass
# a real Postgres URL into `Hecks::Adapters::PostgresEra.connect_for`
# directly, proven working there first.
Hecks.world "QualityControl" do
  persisted_by("PostgresEra") do
    database ENV.fetch("DATABASE_URL")
  end
end
